1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,170
Listen to a talk on a podcast about music.

2
00:00:04,170 --> 00:00:14,140
Today, let's explore the fascinating world of synaesthesia, a condition where one sensory experience involuntarily triggers another.

3
00:00:14,140 --> 00:00:18,130
This phenomenon is particularly intriguing in the realm of music

4
00:00:18,130 --> 00:00:21,230
where certain individuals can see sounds or taste music.

5
00:00:21,230 --> 00:00:25,080
Synaesthetic experiences vary widely among individuals.

6
00:00:25,080 --> 00:00:31,080
For example, some might perceive specific colors when hearing particular musical notes or chords.

7
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:34,420
A person might see red when they hear a C note

8
00:00:34,420 --> 00:00:36,210
or blue when they hear a G note.

9
00:00:36,210 --> 00:00:40,910
This blending of senses can enhance their emotional connection to the music

10
00:00:40,910 --> 00:00:43,220
and even influence their creative processes.

11
00:00:43,220 --> 00:00:50,000
A number of famous musicians, such as Duke Ellington, have reported experiencing synesthesia

12
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,140
which they claim helps them in composing and performing music.

13
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:59,250
Neurologists theorize that synaesthesia stems from heightened neural connections between sensory regions

14
00:00:59,250 --> 00:01:04,140
allowing stimulation of one sense to trigger involuntary experiences with another.

15
00:01:04,140 --> 00:01:15,180
Though uncommon, it offers profound insight into perception and the mechanisms underlying sensory integration, helping researchers explore cognition, memory and even creativity.

16
00:01:15,180 --> 00:01:18,260
Moreover, synesthesia is not limited to music.

17
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:24,022
It can involve other combinations of senses, such as associating certain tastes with numbers

18
00:01:24,022 --> 00:01:26,150
or textures with emotions.

19
00:01:26,150 --> 00:01:31,028
Understanding synaesthesia can help us appreciate the varied ways people experience the world

20
00:01:31,028 --> 00:01:34,120
and highlight the incredible potential of the human brain.
